        "subject": "LU-18823 fld: replicate the full FLDB to every MDT",
        "message": "LU-18823 fld: replicate the full FLDB to every MDT\n\nOnly MDT0000 holds a complete FID Location Database (FLDB).  Each\nother MDT keeps only its own sequence ranges on disk, fetched once\nat mount, and caches other ranges in memory without persisting\nthem, so no MDT other than MDT0000 has a copy that could rebuild\nthe FLDB if MDT0000 were lost.\n\nKeep a full on-disk copy of the FLDB on every MDT:\n\n - fld_server_read() returns the whole FLDB (all MDT and OST ranges)\n   when the request range type is ANY, keeping the batched read.\n - fld_update_from_controller() fetches the entire FLDB at mount on a\n   non-MDT0 MDT instead of only that MDT's own ranges.\n - mdt_register_lwp_callback() no longer skips the one-time FLDB\n   refresh when the super-sequence is still being allocated from the\n   controller (-EINPROGRESS/-EAGAIN) at the connect point.  The\n   FLD_READ RPC is resent by ptlrpc until the controller is ready,\n   so the refresh completes without a local retry loop.\n - a range looked up on demand is persisted with fld_insert_entry()\n   rather than only cached, so ranges allocated after the mount\n   refresh are added to the local FLDB as they are used.\n\nThe on-disk FLDB from any MDT can then be dumped and written back to\nMDT0000 to restore it (see the \"fldb\" parameter).  MDTs run in\nlockstep within a filesystem, so no connect flag is used to\nnegotiate the full read.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=conf-sanity\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-4.8 ltvm,llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I47ebaa6eadbfbdeb57784e0b77ce9e6f683ebbe5\n"
      },
